# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mode-based indexing {#mode-based-indexing}"
msgstr "Indicizzazione basata sui modi {#mode-based-indexing}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "All operator representations refer to the indices that their terms act upon as **modes**. A mode is simply an index identifying a fermionic degree of freedom in your system. The mapping from physical degrees of freedom (such as spatial orbitals, spin states, or other quantum numbers) to mode indices is left to the user, enabling maximum flexibility.\n"
msgstr "Tutte le rappresentazioni degli operatori indicano gli indici su cui agiscono i loro termini come **modi**. Un modo è semplicemente un indice che identifica un grado di libertà fermionico nel tuo sistema. La mappatura dai gradi di libertà fisici (come orbitali spaziali, stati di spin o altri numeri quantici) agli indici dei modi è lasciata all'utente, per garantire la massima flessibilità.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This abstraction is also present in the [`qiskit_fermions.circuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit#module-qiskit_fermions.circuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit\") module, where the [`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") operates on a register of fermionic modes. In both the operator and circuit representations, modes provide a consistent, algebra-independent way to specify which degrees of freedom participate in a given operation.\n"
msgstr "Questa astrazione è presente anche nel modulo [`qiskit_fermions.circuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit#module-qiskit_fermions.circuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit\"), dove [`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") opera su un registro di modi fermionici. Sia nella rappresentazione degli operatori sia in quella dei circuiti, i modi offrono un modo coerente e indipendente dall'algebra per specificare quali gradi di libertà partecipano a una data operazione.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  **Current implementations use spinless modes**: All operator representations currently provided by this module treat modes as spinless fermionic degrees of freedom. This means if your system has both spin-up and spin-down electrons or fermions, you must explicitly map them to distinct modes (for example, modes 0-3 for spin-up of four spatial orbitals, modes 4-7 for spin-down, or any other convention you choose).\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Importante\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  **Le implementazioni attuali usano modi senza spin**: tutte le rappresentazioni degli operatori attualmente fornite da questo modulo trattano i modi come gradi di libertà fermionici privi di spin. Questo significa che, se il tuo sistema ha sia elettroni o fermioni con spin up sia con spin down, devi mapparli esplicitamente su modi distinti (ad esempio, i modi 0-3 per lo spin up di quattro orbitali spaziali, i modi 4-7 per lo spin down, o qualsiasi altra convenzione tu scelga).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  This design keeps the core representations simple and general while avoiding imposing a specific spin-ordering convention. Utility modules like [`qiskit_fermions.operators.library`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library#module-qiskit_fermions.operators.library \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library\") provide convenience functions (for example, [`FCIDump.from_file()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library-fci-dump#qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file\")) that handle such mappings for you when loading electronic structure data.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"  Questo design mantiene semplici e generali le rappresentazioni di base, evitando di imporre una specifica convenzione di ordinamento dello spin. I moduli di utilità come [`qiskit_fermions.operators.library`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library#module-qiskit_fermions.operators.library \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library\") forniscono funzioni di comodo (ad esempio, [`FCIDump.from_file()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library-fci-dump#qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file\")) che gestiscono tali mappature al posto tuo quando carichi dati di struttura elettronica.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  As the package evolves, spinful operator representations might be added that natively support spin degrees of freedom in their data model. These will be clearly distinguished from the current spinless implementations and will coexist with them in the module.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Suggerimento\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Con l'evoluzione del pacchetto, potrebbero essere aggiunte rappresentazioni di operatori con spin che supportano nativamente i gradi di libertà di spin nel loro modello dati. Queste saranno chiaramente distinte dalle attuali implementazioni senza spin e coesisteranno con esse nel modulo.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"term-grouping\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Term grouping and commutation relationships {#term-grouping-and-commutation-relationships}"
msgstr "Raggruppamento dei termini e relazioni di commutazione {#term-grouping-and-commutation-relationships}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Like the coefficients and mode indices, operators can optionally store a **groups array** that associates each term with a group index. By integrating grouping directly into the operator representation as part of the sparse data structure, grouping information naturally travels with the operator through transformations. This enables systematic structure usage, whether from physical properties, algebraic relationships, or problem-specific symmetries. The structured information can then be used in downstream operations like circuit synthesis and decomposition by using methods like [`OperatorTrait.split_out_groups()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups\").\n"
msgstr "Come i coefficienti e gli indici dei modi, gli operatori possono opzionalmente memorizzare un **array di gruppi** che associa ogni termine a un indice di gruppo. Integrando il raggruppamento direttamente nella rappresentazione dell'operatore come parte della struttura dati sparsa, le informazioni di raggruppamento accompagnano naturalmente l'operatore attraverso le trasformazioni. Questo consente di sfruttare la struttura in modo sistematico, che derivi da proprietà fisiche, relazioni algebriche o simmetrie specifiche del problema. Le informazioni strutturate possono poi essere usate in operazioni a valle come la sintesi e la decomposizione dei circuiti, tramite metodi come [`OperatorTrait.split_out_groups()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups\").\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For detailed guidance on how to group operator terms in your workflows, see the [grouping](grouping#grouping-explanation) guide.\n"
msgstr "Per una guida dettagliata su come raggruppare i termini degli operatori nei tuoi flussi di lavoro, consulta la guida al [raggruppamento](grouping#grouping-explanation).\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import MajoranaOperator, gamma\n"
"    >>> op = MajoranaOperator.from_dict({\n"
"    ...     (gamma(0, False),): 1.0,\n"
"    ...     (gamma(1, False),): 1.0,\n"
"    ...     (gamma(2, False), gamma(3, False)): 1.0\n"
"    ... })\n"
"    >>> # Assign group indices to terms\n"
"    >>> op.groups = [0, 0, 1]\n"
"    >>> # Partition operator by groups\n"
"    >>> grouped_ops = op.split_out_groups()\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Create operator with 3 terms\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[3] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 2, 3};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[4] = {0, 1, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *op = qf_maj_op_new(3, 4, coeffs, modes, boundaries);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Create operator with 3 terms\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[3] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 2, 3};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[4] = {0, 1, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *op = qf_maj_op_new(3, 4, coeffs, modes, boundaries);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Assign grouping information\n"
"    uint32_t groups[3] = {0, 0, 1};\n"
"    qf_maj_op_set_groups(op, groups, 3);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Assign grouping information\n"
"    uint32_t groups[3] = {0, 0, 1};\n"
"    qf_maj_op_set_groups(op, groups, 3);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Partition operator by groups\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *grouped_ops[2];\n"
"    qf_maj_op_split_out_groups(op, NULL, 0, grouped_ops);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Partition operator by groups\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *grouped_ops[2];\n"
"    qf_maj_op_split_out_groups(op, NULL, 0, grouped_ops);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"id3\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Arithmetic and mathematical operations {#arithmetic-and-mathematical-operations}"
msgstr "Operazioni aritmetiche e matematiche {#arithmetic-and-mathematical-operations}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "All operators implement the [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") protocol, which provides a unified set of operations across different operator types. This ensures that code written for one operator representation works uniformly with others. See [`qiskit_fermions.protocols`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/protocols#module-qiskit_fermions.protocols \"qiskit_fermions.protocols\") for how this protocol relates to the others this package defines.\n"
msgstr "Tutti gli operatori implementano il protocollo [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\"), che fornisce un insieme unificato di operazioni per i diversi tipi di operatori. Questo garantisce che il codice scritto per una rappresentazione di operatore funzioni in modo uniforme con le altre. Consulta [`qiskit_fermions.protocols`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/protocols#module-qiskit_fermions.protocols \"qiskit_fermions.protocols\") per vedere come questo protocollo si relaziona agli altri definiti da questo pacchetto.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The protocol includes arithmetic operations (such as addition, multiplication, and composition), structural operations (term iteration, mode support analysis, relabeling), mathematical functions (normal ordering, simplification, equivalence checking), and more. See the [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") documentation for a complete reference of all available operations.\n"
msgstr "Il protocollo comprende operazioni aritmetiche (come addizione, moltiplicazione e composizione), operazioni strutturali (iterazione sui termini, analisi del supporto dei modi, rietichettatura), funzioni matematiche (ordinamento normale, semplificazione, verifica di equivalenza) e altro ancora. Consulta la documentazione di [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") per un riferimento completo di tutte le operazioni disponibili.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator, cre, ann\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Construct a Hermitian operator: H = +0 -1 + +1 -0\n"
"    >>> op = FermionOperator.from_dict({\n"
"    ...     (cre(0), ann(1)): 1.0,\n"
"    ...     (cre(1), ann(0)): 1.0\n"
"    ... })\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Check if the operator is Hermitian\n"
"    >>> print(f\"Operator is Hermitian: {op.is_hermitian(atol=1e-10)}\")\n"
"    Operator is Hermitian: True\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Construct a Hermitian operator: H = +0 -1 + +1 -0\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[2] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions[4] = {true, false, true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 1, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[3] = {0, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op = qf_ferm_op_new(2, 4, coeffs, actions, modes, boundaries);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Construct a Hermitian operator: H = +0 -1 + +1 -0\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[2] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions[4] = {true, false, true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 1, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[3] = {0, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op = qf_ferm_op_new(2, 4, coeffs, actions, modes, boundaries);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Check if the operator is Hermitian\n"
"    bool is_hermitian = qf_ferm_op_is_hermitian(op, 1e-10);\n"
"    printf(\"Operator is Hermitian: %s\\n\", is_hermitian ? \"true\" : \"false\");\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Check if the operator is Hermitian\n"
"    bool is_hermitian = qf_ferm_op_is_hermitian(op, 1e-10);\n"
"    printf(\"Operator is Hermitian: %s\\n\", is_hermitian ? \"true\" : \"false\");\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Clean up\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Clean up\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<DoqAdmonition type=\"note\" title=\"Note\">\n"
msgstr "<DoqAdmonition type=\"note\" title=\"Nota\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "  [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") verifies that $H - H^\\dagger = 0$ by reducing that difference to a normal form and comparing it against [`zero()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero\"). Spelled out in terms of the other protocol operations, it performs the following:\n"
msgstr "  [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") verifica che $H - H^\\dagger = 0$ riducendo tale differenza a una forma normale e confrontandola con [`zero()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero\"). Espresso in termini delle altre operazioni del protocollo, esegue quanto segue:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "  The strength of this check depends on the operator type, because it depends on how completely that type’s normal form contracts adjacent generators. A `True` result is always reliable, but for some types a `False` result is conservative: see [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") for details.\n"
msgstr "  L'affidabilità di questo controllo dipende dal tipo di operatore, perché dipende da quanto completamente la forma normale di quel tipo contrae i generatori adiacenti. Un risultato `True` è sempre affidabile, ma per alcuni tipi un risultato `False` è prudenziale: consulta [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") per i dettagli.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "</DoqAdmonition>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  The example uses `atol=1e-10`. The `atol` (absolute tolerance) parameter specifies a threshold. Coefficients with magnitude smaller than `atol` are treated as zero and discarded. This is essential for numerical stability when comparing operators, since floating-point arithmetic can introduce small rounding errors that would otherwise prevent equivalent operators from being recognized as such.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Importante\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  L'esempio usa `atol=1e-10`. Il parametro `atol` (tolleranza assoluta) specifica una soglia. I coefficienti con modulo inferiore a `atol` sono trattati come zero e scartati. Questo è essenziale per la stabilità numerica nel confronto tra operatori, poiché l'aritmetica in virgola mobile può introdurre piccoli errori di arrotondamento che altrimenti impedirebbero di riconoscere come tali operatori equivalenti.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  While the [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") protocol provides a common interface, individual operator implementations might offer additional convenience methods not part of the protocol. For example, [`FermionOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-fermion-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator\") and [`MajoranaOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-majorana-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator\") provide a `max_rank()` method that reports the length of their longest term. Always consult the API documentation for your operator type to discover all available functionality.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Suggerimento\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Sebbene il protocollo [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") fornisca un'interfaccia comune, le singole implementazioni di operatori potrebbero offrire metodi di comodo aggiuntivi che non fanno parte del protocollo. Ad esempio, [`FermionOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-fermion-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator\") e [`MajoranaOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-majorana-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator\") forniscono un metodo `max_rank()` che restituisce la lunghezza del loro termine più lungo. Consulta sempre la documentazione API del tuo tipo di operatore per scoprire tutte le funzionalità disponibili.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"operator-term-ordering\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Operator term ordering and normal forms {#operator-term-ordering-and-normal-forms}"
msgstr "Ordinamento dei termini degli operatori e forme normali {#operator-term-ordering-and-normal-forms}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A fundamental challenge in quantum operator algebra is that mathematically equivalent operators can be represented in many different ways, each with different implications for quantum algorithms. The same operator can be written in algebraically equivalent forms (for example, $a^\\dagger b$ can be expressed as $ba^\\dagger + [a^\\dagger,b]$), yet these representations lead to different behavior in circuit synthesis, simplification, and numerical algorithms.\n"
msgstr "Una sfida fondamentale nell'algebra degli operatori quantistici è che operatori matematicamente equivalenti possono essere rappresentati in molti modi diversi, ciascuno con implicazioni diverse per gli algoritmi quantistici. Lo stesso operatore può essere scritto in forme algebricamente equivalenti (ad esempio, $a^\\dagger b$ può essere espresso come $ba^\\dagger + [a^\\dagger,b]$), eppure queste rappresentazioni portano a comportamenti diversi nella sintesi dei circuiti, nella semplificazione e negli algoritmi numerici.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The operator representations support **normal ordering** operations that transform operators into algebra-specific canonical forms using commutation relations. This enables reliable comparisons (two equivalent operators have identical normal-ordered forms), reveals simplifications (commutation relations cause terms to cancel or combine), and supports algorithms that require specific operator forms for correctness and efficiency.\n"
msgstr "Le rappresentazioni degli operatori supportano operazioni di **ordinamento normale** che trasformano gli operatori in forme canoniche specifiche dell'algebra usando le relazioni di commutazione. Questo consente confronti affidabili (due operatori equivalenti hanno forme ordinate normalmente identiche), rivela semplificazioni (le relazioni di commutazione fanno cancellare o combinare i termini) e supporta algoritmi che richiedono forme specifiche dell'operatore per correttezza ed efficienza.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  Different operator representations might implement normal ordering based on different commutation relations appropriate to their algebra. For example, fermionic normal ordering uses anticommutation relations ($\\{c_i, c_j^\\dagger\\} = \\delta_{ij}$), while Majorana normal ordering uses different algebra conventions ($\\{\\gamma_i, \\gamma_j\\} = 2\\delta_{ij}$). Always consult the documentation for your operator type to understand how normal ordering is implemented.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Importante\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  Rappresentazioni di operatori diverse potrebbero implementare l'ordinamento normale basandosi su diverse relazioni di commutazione appropriate alla loro algebra. Ad esempio, l'ordinamento normale fermionico usa relazioni di anticommutazione ($\\{c_i, c_j^\\dagger\\} = \\delta_{ij}$), mentre l'ordinamento normale di Majorana usa convenzioni algebriche diverse ($\\{\\gamma_i, \\gamma_j\\} = 2\\delta_{ij}$). Consulta sempre la documentazione del tuo tipo di operatore per capire come è implementato l'ordinamento normale.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator, cre, ann\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Two different representations of the same operator\n"
"    >>> op1 = FermionOperator.from_dict({(ann(0), cre(0)): 1.0})\n"
"    >>> op2 = FermionOperator.from_dict({(): 1.0, (cre(0), ann(0)): -1.0})\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Direct comparison fails due to different forms\n"
"    >>> op1.equiv(op2, atol=1e-10)\n"
"    False\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Normal-order both and compare again\n"
"    >>> op1_normal = op1.normal_ordered()\n"
"    >>> op2_normal = op2.normal_ordered()\n"
"    >>> op1_normal.equiv(op2_normal, atol=1e-10)\n"
"    True\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```c\n"
"    #include <qiskit_fermions.h>\n"
"    #include <stdbool.h>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Two different representations of the same operator\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeff1[1] = {{1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions1[2] = {false, true};\n"
"    uint32_t modes1[2] = {0, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries1[2] = {0, 2};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op1 = qf_ferm_op_new(1, 2, coeff1, actions1, modes1, boundaries1);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Two different representations of the same operator\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeff1[1] = {{1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions1[2] = {false, true};\n"
"    uint32_t modes1[2] = {0, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries1[2] = {0, 2};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op1 = qf_ferm_op_new(1, 2, coeff1, actions1, modes1, boundaries1);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
